GNU findutils for OpenVMS
=========================

This is GNU findutils @VERSION@ (find and xargs) built natively for OpenVMS
@ARCH@, from the unmodified release plus the patches in the vms-findutils
project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-findutils

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  GNU findutils is free software under the GNU
General Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  The manuals are
FIND.1 and XARGS.1, also as plain text in FIND.TXT and XARGS.TXT.

Files
-----
    FINDUTILS$ROOT:[BIN]FIND.EXE
    FINDUTILS$ROOT:[BIN]XARGS.EXE
    FINDUTILS$ROOT:[DOC]                          this file, COPYING, NEWS, the manuals
    FINDUTILS$ROOT:[000000]FINDUTILS$SETUP.COM    defines the find and xargs commands
    SYS$STARTUP:FINDUTILS$STARTUP.COM             defines FINDUTILS$ROOT

Setup
-----
The kit installs FINDUTILS$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.
To define FINDUTILS$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:FINDUTILS$STARTUP.COM

Each user then defines the commands (for example in LOGIN.COM):

    $ @FINDUTILS$ROOT:[000000]FINDUTILS$SETUP.COM

Use
---
    $ find . -name "*.c"                          ! Unix names: ./src/main.c
    $ find [] -name "*.c"                         ! VMS names:  [.src]main.c
    $ find [] -name "*.log" -exec delete {};* ;   ! DCL verbs work with VMS names
    $ pipe find . -name "*.c" | xargs grep -l main

(Under the traditional DCL parse style, quote options with capital
letters, or SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED.)

-exec, -execdir, -ok and xargs run the command as DCL in a subprocess, so
DCL verbs, foreign-command symbols and @procedures all work.  The words you
type are DCL syntax, so qualifiers work; file names are quoted unless they
are VMS file specifications.

A starting point in VMS syntax ([], [.dir], DKA0:[X], dir.dir) gives VMS
names ([.dir.sub]file.txt, a directory as [.dir]sub.DIR); a Unix one (.,
dir) gives Unix names.

Known limits
------------
- locate, updatedb and frcode are not included in this release.
- xargs -P N runs one command at a time.
- One subprocess per command: -exec ... ; over many files is slow; use
  -exec ... + or xargs.
- A command's arguments are limited to about 4000 bytes (DCL's limit);
  find and xargs split longer lists over several commands.
- A @procedure gets unquoted parameters upper-cased by DCL.
- -fstype reports "unknown".  With a VMS starting point, -printf's %h, %P
  and %f still give Unix forms.
- > on the command line is not redirected; use PIPE.
